<p>Delta ratings are manufacturer figures for light products (ABB IRB 365, Omron Quattro). Rated speed for your product, tray pattern, and changeover time should come from each supplier. EOAT = end-of-arm tooling. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="unload-heading">Automatic Tray Unloaders &amp; Destackers</h2>
<p>An automatic tray unloader removes product from trays, or separates stacked trays, without manual handling. Unloading shows up wherever trays are a carrier rather than the final package: returnable trays and crates from a cooler or freezer, bakery trays feeding a wrapper, and component trays feeding an assembly or filling line. The method depends on whether the product can tolerate a drop and whether the empty trays return to the line.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>How It Works</th>
<th>Suits</th>
<th>Watch For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Tray Inversion (Tipping)</td>
<td>Clamps and rotates the tray to dump product onto a conveyor or into a hopper</td>
<td>Robust or bulk product: frozen pieces, produce, loose parts</td>
<td>Drop height and impact damage, product sticking to the tray</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pick-Off (Robot or Gantry)</td>
<td>Vacuum or gripper tooling lifts product from tray cavities, the reverse of tray loading</td>
<td>Fragile or oriented product that must keep its position</td>
<td>Cavity-to-cavity position variation, tooling changeover per tray format</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Push-Off / Sweep</td>
<td>A sweep bar slides product off a flat tray or sheet onto a conveyor</td>
<td>Flat-bottomed product on flat trays or sheets</td>
<td>Product skewing or stacking as it transfers, tray surface friction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tray Destacking</td>
<td>Separates a stack of full or empty trays into single trays on a conveyor</td>
<td>Returnable trays and crates, feeding an unloader or a tray washer</td>
<td>Warped or damaged trays that jam, mixed tray types in one stack</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Destacking is different from denesting. A denester separates nested, disposable trays that sit inside each other. A destacker separates stackable trays and crates that rest on each other. Tray inversion hardware overlaps with the turners and inverters covered on the <a href="/conveyor-functions/product-handling/">product handling</a> page.</p>
<h3>Horticulture Tray Loaders: Pots and Baskets</h3>
<p>In nurseries and greenhouses, "trayloader" and "tray unloader" usually mean potting-line equipment that places filled plant pots into carrier (shuttle) trays or takes them out. The building blocks match packaging lines: destackers, pot and tray conveyors, and grouping tables. <a href="https://www.urbinati.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Urbinati</a>, for example, lists automatic destackers "for trays, pots and shuttle trays" and buffer tables "for the grouping and positioning of pots and trays." Filling machines sit upstream: <a href="https://packmfg.com/standard-equipment/potting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pack Manufacturing</a> describes its PM1100 as filling "nursery pots, hanging baskets, flats and plug trays," and <a href="https://mayer.de/en/planting-systems/products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayer</a> rates its KF 2260 tray filler at up to 1,600 trays per hour. For pot and basket loading, specify pot diameter and shape, tray cell pattern, plant height and foliage spread, and whether trays return empty to the potting line.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/tray-urbinati-nd-pot-destacker.webp" width="870" height="295" alt="Two views of a destacker dropping fibre plant pots from tall stacks into black carrier trays moving on a conveyor." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Urbinati ND destacker placing biodegradable pots into carrier trays. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.urbinati.com/en/product/automatic-destacker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Urbinati</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<h3>Bakery &amp; Snack Foods</h3>
<p>Bakery lines load cookies, crackers, pastries, bars, and breadsticks into thermoformed trays before over-wrapping or flow-wrapping. Products arrive from the oven conveyor at high speed and often in random orientation. Delta robot cells with vision guidance handle the random infeed without upstream singulation. Cam-driven systems serve dedicated cookie lines running a single product at high rates. </p>
<h3>Fresh Produce</h3>
<p>Produce tray loading handles berries, cherry tomatoes, mushrooms, and salad components. The primary challenge is product fragility, berries bruise under even moderate gripping force. Vacuum pick-and-place with soft silicone cups and controlled vacuum pressure provides the gentlest handling. Produce lines also require full <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary washdown construction</a> for daily cleaning. Low-backpressure accumulation, where product slips on the conveyor instead of pushing against the product ahead, protects delicate produce at stops.</p>
<h3>Ready Meals &amp; Prepared Foods</h3>
<p>Ready-meal tray loading places main courses, sides, and sauces into compartmented CPET or aluminum trays. Products vary in shape, consistency, and temperature, cold salads, room-temperature grains, and hot proteins load into different compartments of the same tray. Multi-head servo gantry systems with independent pick modules handle the different product streams. The outfeed conveyor delivers loaded trays to modified-atmosphere packaging (MAP) sealers or top-seal machines.</p>
<h3>Meat &amp; Poultry</h3>
<p>Meat tray loading places cuts, portions, or formed products into foam or rigid trays for retail overwrap or MAP sealing. The challenge is handling wet, irregular product in a refrigerated room with aggressive daily sanitation. Stainless steel frames, washdown-rated drives, and quick-disconnect conveyor sections are standard expectations. Many suppliers offer tray loading integrated with sealing for meat processing.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Choose an Automatic Tray Loader</h2>
<p>Choose an automatic tray loader by answering four questions, which set the technology, size, and cost of the system:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is your throughput requirement?</strong> Measure in products per minute and trays per minute (products per tray times trays per minute). A line placing 8 cookies per tray at 30 trays/minute requires 240 placements/minute. Compare that figure against each supplier's rated speed for your product, not a brochure maximum.</li>
<li><strong>How many SKUs and how often do you change over?</strong> Lines running a few SKUs with infrequent changeovers favor cam-driven. Lines running many SKUs with frequent changeovers need robotic or servo gantry with recipe-based changeover.</li>
<li><strong>What is the product's fragility and shape consistency?</strong> Fragile products (decorated cakes, fresh berries) need controlled-force gripping, delta robots with force-limited vacuum cups. Uniform, robust products (granola bars, frozen entrees) handle mechanical pick-and-place well.</li>
<li><strong>What is your environment and sanitation requirement?</strong> Full daily washdown (bakery, meat) calls for stainless steel construction, washdown-rated drives, and tool-free belt removal. Dry ambient environments (confectionery, snack) accept standard painted steel frames and enclosed drives.</li>
</ol>
<p>Beyond these four questions, consider upstream and downstream integration. The tray loader does not operate in isolation, it depends on a reliable product infeed (often an <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a>) and a buffered outfeed (often an <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a>). Include the surrounding conveyor infrastructure in the project budget, since it is a significant share of total cost and is often quoted separately from the loader.</p>
<p>For detailed cost factors across all conveyor types, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost and pricing guide</a>.</p>
<SpecChecklist id="spec-checklist" title="Tray loader and unloader specification checklist" items={[
  ["Product", "Dimensions, weight range, fragility, surface (dry, sticky, frozen, wet), and whether orientation matters in the tray."],
  ["Trays", "Material, cavity layout, nested or stacked supply, flange dimensions, and every tray format the machine must run."],
  ["Rates", "Trays per minute and products per tray at current and planned peak volume."],
  ["Infeed condition", "Random or spaced and oriented product, and what upstream equipment feeds the cell."],
  ["SKUs and changeover", "Number of SKUs, changeovers per shift, target changeover time, and which tooling changes per SKU."],
  ["Tray supply", "Denester or destacker magazine capacity, reload method, and whether trays arrive nested or stacked."],
  ["Unloading", "Whether product can tolerate a drop, and whether empty trays go to a washer, restacker, or back to the loader."],
  ["Sanitation", "Dry clean or full washdown, required ingress rating, and food-contact material requirements."],
  ["Integration", "Downstream sealer, lidder, or wrapper speed, outfeed buffer space, and line controls interface."],
  ["Safety", "Guarding and access for robot cells and point-of-operation guarding for pick heads."],
  ["Footprint", "Available floor space, ceiling height for overhead robots, and access for magazine reloads and maintenance."],
  ["Acceptance test", "Run test with your product and trays at rated speed, allowed mispick and empty-cavity rate, and measured changeover time."]
]} />
<p>OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/robotics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">robotics overview</a> notes there are no robot-specific OSHA standards and covers hazard recognition for robot cells. General machine guarding falls under <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a>.</p>
<p>To shortlist builders once the spec is written, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-suppliers/">conveyor suppliers guide</a>.</p>
