<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is an automatic tray loader?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An automatic tray loader places product into trays without manual labor. The system combines a tray denester, product infeed conveyor, placement mechanism (pick-and-place, servo gantry, or delta robot), and outfeed conveyor. Speed depends on the placement mechanism, product, and tray pattern.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is an automatic tray unloader?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An automatic tray unloader removes product from trays, or separates stacked trays, without manual handling. The four common methods are tray inversion (tipping the tray to dump product), pick-off by robot or gantry, push-off or sweep for flat trays, and tray destacking, which separates a stack of full or empty trays into single trays on a conveyor. The right method depends on whether the product can tolerate a drop and whether the trays return to the line for reuse.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between pick-and-place and robotic tray loading?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pick-and-place systems use fixed-motion mechanisms, cam-driven or servo gantry, that follow repeatable paths at high speed. Robotic systems use delta or articulated robots with programmable motion paths and vision guidance. Pick-and-place suits high-speed, low-SKU operations. Robotic suits high-SKU, frequent-changeover environments where random product infeed is common.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How fast can an automatic tray loader run?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cam-driven pick-and-place is the fastest option for a single small, uniform product. Servo gantry trades some speed for recipe-based changeover. Manufacturers rate single delta robots at roughly 120 to 300 picks per minute for light products: ABB rates its IRB 365 at 120 with a 1 kg product, and Omron rated its Quattro at 300. Multi-robot cells add capacity with more robots. Actual speed depends on product size, weight, fragility, and tray configuration.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What industries use automatic tray loading?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakery and snack foods (cookies, crackers, bars), fresh produce (berries, tomatoes), ready meals and prepared foods, meat and poultry, confectionery, and pharmaceutical blister packing all use automatic tray loading. Any operation placing discrete products into thermoformed or pre-formed trays benefits from automation when manual crews cannot keep up with line rate or when labor availability is constrained.</p>

</div>
